The baseline uses six ciphertext multiplies and depth 8. This version uses four. It applies the identity T5 = 2·T2·T3 − T1 and T7 = 2·T3·T4 − T1, so the odd high-order terms collapse into one ciphertext product T3·V, where V = 2(c5·T2 + c7·T4) + c3. The linear part c1·z − c5·z − c7·z and the constant c0 are added at the end using only plaintext multiplies and adds. The expected depth is about 5, and the terms that meet in each sum have different multiplication histories or sit at different levels, so the scale-matching rule is respected.

```cpp
// Chebyshev hardshrink degree 7 on [-4, 4].
// Uses T5 = 2*T2*T3 - T1 and T7 = 2*T3*T4 - T1 so that
// c5*T5 + c7*T7 + c3*T3 = T3 * (2*(c5*T2 + c7*T4) + c3) + (c5+c7)*(-z).
// Only 4 ciphertext-ciphertext multiplies (z^2, z*T2, T2^2, T3*V), depth ~5.

void fhe()
{
  Ciphertext x("x");
  Plaintext alpha("alpha");
  Plaintext one("one");
  Plaintext c0("c0");
  Plaintext c1("c1");
  Plaintext c3("c3");
  Plaintext c5("c5");
  Plaintext c7("c7");

  Ciphertext z = x * alpha;          // z in [-1, 1]

  Ciphertext z2 = z * z;
  Ciphertext T2 = z2 + z2 - one;     // T2 = 2z^2 - 1

  Ciphertext zT2 = z * T2;
  Ciphertext T3 = zT2 + zT2 - z;     // T3 = 2 z T2 - z

  Ciphertext T2sq = T2 * T2;
  Ciphertext T4 = T2sq + T2sq - one; // T4 = 2 T2^2 - 1

  Ciphertext W = T2 * c5 + T4 * c7;  // c5*T2 + c7*T4
  Ciphertext V = W + W + c3;         // 2W + c3

  Ciphertext prod = T3 * V;          // T3 * (2c5T2 + 2c7T4 + c3)

  Ciphertext lin = z * c1 - z * c5 - z * c7;  // (c1 - c5 - c7) z

  Ciphertext result = prod + lin + c0;
  result.set_output("y");
}
```

Attempt 1 is already at 4 ciphertext multiplies, which is close to the minimum for a degree-7 evaluation. A Paterson–Stockmeyer-style split (z², z³, z⁴ then one block product) also needs 4. The remaining cost is mostly the ciphertext multiplies plus level alignment. In this variant I fold c0 into the cheap low-level linear part, so one plaintext add happens at low level and the final sum is just `prod + lin`. This is a small change and may only give a marginal gain.

```cpp
// Chebyshev hardshrink degree 7 on [-4, 4].
// T5 = 2*T2*T3 - T1, T7 = 2*T3*T4 - T1, T4 = 2*T2^2 - 1.
// p = T3 * (2*(c5*T2 + c7*T4) + c3) + (c1 - c5 - c7) z + c0.
// 4 ciphertext-ciphertext multiplies (z^2, z*T2, T2^2, T3*V), depth ~5.

void fhe()
{
  Ciphertext x("x");
  Plaintext alpha("alpha");
  Plaintext one("one");
  Plaintext c0("c0");
  Plaintext c1("c1");
  Plaintext c3("c3");
  Plaintext c5("c5");
  Plaintext c7("c7");

  Ciphertext z = x * alpha;          // z in [-1, 1]

  // Low-level linear part first (cheap, shallow level).
  Ciphertext lin = z * c1 - z * c5 - z * c7 + c0;

  Ciphertext z2 = z * z;
  Ciphertext T2 = z2 + z2 - one;     // T2 = 2z^2 - 1

  Ciphertext zT2 = z * T2;
  Ciphertext T3 = zT2 + zT2 - z;     // T3 = 2 z T2 - z

  Ciphertext T2sq = T2 * T2;
  Ciphertext T4 = T2sq + T2sq - one; // T4 = 2 T2^2 - 1

  Ciphertext W = T2 * c5 + T4 * c7;  // c5*T2 + c7*T4
  Ciphertext V = W + W + c3;         // 2W + c3

  Ciphertext prod = T3 * V;

  Ciphertext result = prod + lin;
  result.set_output("y");
}
```

Attempt 2 reordered the linear part and came out slower (319.7 ms vs 284.0 ms) with the same operation counts, so that change didn't help. I'm returning to the attempt 1 kernel, which is the best measured so far. Its four ciphertext multiplies are close to the minimum for degree 7, and I don't see a cheaper restructuring that avoids the remaining plaintext multiplies or level alignments.

